----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NFW benchmark: M_500 = 1.000e+15 Msun at z_l = 0.5, z_s = 2.0
  -> R_500 = 1.293 Mpc, theta_500 = 3.425', M_vir = 1.7945e+15 Msun, c_vir = 3.503
  beam = 15.00" = 0.250', theta_500 = 3.425'  -> theta_500 / FWHM = 13.70

 r/theta500  r [arcmin]     <mu>   <ln mu>     beams  peaks/cl
       0.50       1.713   3.0707    0.6903     130.1      32.5
       1.00       3.425   1.6572    0.2994     520.5     130.1
       1.50       5.138   1.3283    0.1680    1171.0     292.8
       2.00       6.850   1.1917    0.1016    2081.8     520.5

  peaks/cluster = pi r^2 / (4 Omega_beam): declustered maxima occur at ~1 per 4 beam solid angles, not 1 per beam.

  theta_500 / FWHM = 13.7: the benchmark cluster spans ~14 beams,
  so even the r < 0.5 theta_500 aperture — where <mu> = 3.07 — is well resolved.  This
  is the structural advantage of the CCAT arm over both others.
